//==
// # Transform interfaces
//
// The interfaces that mark a value as carryable through a transform:
// ITransformable2D/3D for the general transform of a dimension, IDeformable2D/3D
// for an arbitrary point mapping. Deformable inherits Transformable — an
// arbitrary point mapping subsumes any named transform — so implementing Deform
// is the only obligation a shape has, and Transform arrives derived.
// Also the interfaces that mark a transform representation by the canonical form
// it converts into: IAffine2D/IAffine3D for the general affine transform of a
// dimension, IRotational3D for the unit quaternion.
// A transform maps points to points. The representations themselves are declared
// in the `transforms-*.plato` files (pose, TRS, affine/projective, frames,
// motor) and their operations in the matching `transforms-*.library.plato`
// files; the derived bodies for the interfaces below live in
// `intervals.library.plato` and
// `intervals-transforms-deformable.library.plato`. The shared conventions of
// the whole transform surface are stated once here.

//==
// # Transform operations
//
// Conversions between every transform representation, application to points and
// vectors (`p.Transform(t)`), composition, inverses, and identity statics.
//
// Conventions used throughout:
// - Matrices are row-vector style (v * M), matching System.Numerics: the product
//   a.Multiply(b) applies a first, then b.
// - Quaternion Multiply is the Hamilton product (a.Multiply(b) applies b first);
//   Concatenate(a, b) applies a first, then b. Rotors and motors follow the
//   quaternion convention through their isomorphisms.
// - Compose(first, second) means "apply first, then second" on EVERY type, so
//   users never need to remember which product order a representation uses.
// - A function named after a type is a conversion; each is total on its stated
//   precondition (e.g. Pose3D(Matrix4x4) requires a rigid matrix).
// - EulerAngles: Yaw is about Y, Pitch about X, Roll about Z; RotationOrder
//   lists the axes in application order (first-applied first), about fixed
//   world axes.

// Can be carried through any transform of the plane, yielding a new Self.
// Transform2D is the widest 2D map (`transforms.types.plato`), so every other
// representation reaches this one member by converting up to it.
interface ITransformable2D
{
    Transform(x: Self, transform: Transform2D): Self;
}

// Can be carried through any transform of space, yielding a new Self.
// Transform3D is the widest 3D map (`transforms.types.plato`), so every other
// representation reaches this one member by converting up to it.
interface ITransformable3D
{
    Transform(x: Self, transform: Transform3D): Self;
}

// Can be deformed by an arbitrary 2D point mapping. Strictly stronger than
// ITransformable2D — a named transform is one particular point mapping — so the
// Transform member is derived from Deform in `intervals.library.plato` and an
// implementor supplies Deform alone.
interface IDeformable2D
    inherits ITransformable2D
{
    Deform(x: Self, mapping: Function1<Point2D, Point2D>): Self;
}

// Can be deformed by an arbitrary 3D point mapping. Strictly stronger than
// ITransformable3D, and its Transform member is likewise derived from Deform.
interface IDeformable3D
    inherits ITransformable3D
{
    Deform(x: Self, mapping: Function1<Point3D, Point3D>): Self;
}

//==
// # Common-ground interfaces
//
// The interfaces that mark a transform representation by the canonical form it
// converts into. Every named 2D or 3D transform reaches the general affine
// transform of its dimension (`AffineTransform2D` / `AffineTransform3D` in
// `transforms.types.plato`), so composition between representations of
// different kinds is defined by converting both sides to that common ground;
// every 3D rotation representation reaches the unit `Quaternion`, the canonical
// rotation form. As stated above, a member named after a type is a conversion.
//
// The interfaces narrow as they inherit: IRotational3D is a IRigid3D is an IAffine3D.
// State the narrowest one a type satisfies — a rigid transform composes and
// inverts exactly, where the affine form only promises a matrix.

// Converts to the general 2D affine transform: the common ground for composing
// 2D transforms of different kinds.
// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affine_transformation
interface IAffine2D
{
    AffineTransform2D(x: Self): AffineTransform2D;
}

// Converts to the general 3D affine transform: the common ground for composing
// 3D transforms of different kinds.
// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affine_transformation
interface IAffine3D
{
    AffineTransform3D(x: Self): AffineTransform3D;
}

// An orientation-preserving isometry of the plane: converts to a rigid pose.
// Distances and angles survive it, and the family is closed under composition
// and inversion, neither of which holds for IAffine2D.
// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rigid_transformation
interface IRigid2D
    inherits IAffine2D
{
    Pose2D(x: Self): Pose2D;
}

// An orientation-preserving isometry of space: converts to a rigid pose.
// Reflections are isometries but are NOT rigid here — they reverse
// orientation — and no scaling or shear qualifies.
// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rigid_transformation
interface IRigid3D
    inherits IAffine3D
{
    Pose3D(x: Self): Pose3D;
}

// A 3D rotation representation: converts to a unit quaternion, the canonical
// form every 3D rotation shares. A rotation is the rigid transform whose
// translation is zero, hence the inheritance.
// https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quaternions_and_spatial_rotation
interface IRotational3D
    inherits IRigid3D
{
    Quaternion(x: Self): Quaternion;
}
